From: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 00:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] VideoPlayer: wait for the subtitle translation after a seek
 or a language change

After a seek, a subtitle opened to be translated, or the translation
turned to another language (which opens the subtitle again and seeks
back to where playback is), playback went on as soon as the video and
audio were ready, and the first lines of the translation came late:
shown when they arrived, not when they were due.

Playback now stays in its buffering, with Kodi's busy wheel and a
"Translating subtitles..." notification, until the translation is 15 s
ahead of it or translated as far as its cues are known
(MODEL::CLead::Check), for at most 30 s after the last seek or opening
it waits for; then it goes on and lines still to come are shown late,
as before. Switching the subtitle off, or to another track, ends the
wait: the gate no longer applies, or the lead it waited for is closed
with its stream. MODEL::CTranslationWait says when a wait starts, goes
on, ends or gives up, and is tested natively; AI::HoldForTranslation
logs each (how long, how far ahead) and tells the start. One wait is
told once: it goes on however seldom the player asks (before the start
sync it does not), and is taken for over only once not asked about for
30 s.

A subtitle file beside the video is opened again without the seek back
an embedded subtitle gets, so playback would not stop to wait: turning
its translation to another language (AI::NextTranslation) seeks to
where playback is as well.

The wait is CVideoPlayer::HoldForSubtitleTranslation(), asked first
thing in SetCaching where the player leaves its buffering
(CACHESTATE_INIT or FULL to PLAY or DONE), before any of SetCaching's
own bookkeeping, so a refused call changes nothing. It never pauses
playback that runs: a pause in the middle of the film would cut the
sound of a passthrough stream. A lead is waited for only soon after
its stream opened or a seek moved it, so buffering later in the film
for a stalled source does not wait for the translation either. It is
not taken for a player stopping, at the end of the input (the streams
no longer inited), while the last title plays out before the next,
for a realtime input (live TV, timeshift), in a disc menu or a still
picture, or when no subtitle is shown.

The player passes its own clock, as it shows the cues (see Process):
for a stream's cues the clock moved by the subtitle delay, for a
file's that plus the title's time offset - its display time would be
off on a disc and across EDL cuts. The clock is put where playback
goes only as the streams are put on it: until then the player waits
if a lead may still be waited for, and decides once the start sync
has placed the clock. The start of a film asks at the position it
starts from, since the clock is only placed later. The subtitle side
never asks the player.

While playback waits, the player's buffers are full and it reads
nothing: the read-ahead catching up after a seek reads at full speed
then, at twice playback's pace only once playback runs, or 15 s of
cues would take most of the wait to read. The wheel is the one Estuary
shows above a cache level of zero, set while the wait holds.

A film's start is out of its buffering before its streams start: the
speed request Prepare() queues sets the caching states done directly,
so SetCaching was never asked. When the start sync is about to place
the clock and the translation is waited for at that position, the
player goes back into its buffering (CACHESTATE_INIT, the stream
players paused, as after a seek) and SetCaching holds it there.

Shared code: the player's buffering state lives only in
CVideoPlayer::SetCaching and UpdatePlayState, its start sync only in
HandlePlaySpeed.

Signed-off-by: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>

